"""Private Chemistry review, normalized records 601–640. No shared bank writes."""
import copy,json
from pathlib import Path
ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[4]; OUT=Path(__file__).parent
qs=[q for q in json.loads((ROOT/'ops/jamb/source-pool.json').read_text(encoding='utf8'))['questions'] if q['subject']=='chemistry'][600:640]
RX='https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/4-2-classifying-chemical-reactions'
GAS='https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/9-4-effusion-and-diffusion-of-gases'
EL='https://openstax.org/books/chemistry/pages/17-7-electrolysis'
REB='https://elearning.reb.rw/pluginfile.php/11670/mod_resource/content/1/S3%20Chemistry%20SB.pdf'
rows=[]
def page(q):
 if q['year']==1997:return 50
 if q['year']==1998:return 53
 if q['year']==2000:return 57
 return 53 if q['num']<=5 else 54 if q['num']<=21 else 55

def yes(i,a,e,urls,*,question=None,options=None,year=None,num=None,audit='',check=None):
 q=qs[i-601];u=copy.deepcopy(q)
 if year:u['year']=year
 if num:u['num']=num
 if question:u['question']=question
 if options:u['options']=dict(zip('ABCDE',options))
 u['answer']=a;u['ai_explanation']=e;u['has_diagram']=False;u['format']=len(u['options'])
 rows.append({'id':q['id'],'before':q,'update':u,'source_pdf_page':page(u),'actual_source_year':u['year'],'actual_source_number':u['num'],'publication_candidate':True,'review_method':'ai-calculation-checked' if check else 'ai-source-checked','independent_reasoning':e,'source_urls':urls,'source_checked_at':'2026-09-12','private_repair_history':audit or 'Compared complete question and options against the supplied PDF; independently solved and wrote a teaching explanation.','calculation_check':check})
def no(i,why,*,year=None,answer=None):
 q=qs[i-601];temp={**q,'year':year or q['year']}
 rows.append({'id':q['id'],'before':q,'update':None,'source_pdf_page':page(temp),'actual_source_year':temp['year'],'actual_source_number':temp['num'],'publication_candidate':False,'hold_reason':why,'independently_indicated_answer':answer,'source_checked_at':'2026-09-12'})
ORG='https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/20-1-hydrocarbons'
CARB='https://edu.rsc.org/resources/carbonyl-chemistry-16-18/4010284.article'
ACID='https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/14-2-ph-and-poh'
CAT='https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/12-7-catalysis'

yes(601,'B','The longest chain containing the OH group has five carbons. The OH group is at carbon 3 whichever end is used, so choose the direction that gives the methyl substituent position 2 rather than 4. The name is 2-methylpentan-3-ol.',[CARB,ORG],question='What is the IUPAC name of CH₃CH₂CH(OH)CH(CH₃)₂?',options=['4-Methylpentan-3-ol','2-Methylpentan-3-ol','3-Methylpentan-3-ol','1,1-Dimethylbutan-2-ol'],audit='Restored OH attached to the third carbon directly from PDF53 structure. Corrected oldD→B; five-carbon OH-containing chain and methyl tie-break independently checked.',check='alcohol-naming')
no(602,'PDF53 options have extra carbon atoms, missing bonds and alkynes inconsistent with one-water dehydration of butan1ol. Exact intended alkene option cannot be established; no guessed bond insertion or carbon deletion. Hold for intact original product structures.')
yes(603,'B','Ethene monomers join by addition polymerisation. Their double bonds open to form the repeating unit –CH₂–CH₂–, giving polyethene, commonly called polythene.',[ORG,REB],question='Ethene undergoes addition polymerisation with oxygen used as an initiator: nCH₂=CH₂ → [–CH₂–CH₂–]ₙ. Which material is produced?',options=['Rubber','Polythene','Polystyrene','Butane'],audit='Restored repeat-unit bracket and subscript from explicit ethene polymerisation premise; no new monomer identity.',check='ethene-repeat')
no(604,'Formula is C₄H₆, which can be an alkyne, diene or cycloalkene. Old alkene answer is wrong, but formula alone cannot uniquely establish alkyne either. Need triple-bond or homologous-series premise rather than infer from available options.',answer='C₄H₆; D consistent but not unique')
yes(605,'D','Complete combustion converts the carbon atoms of a hydrocarbon into carbon dioxide and its hydrogen atoms into water. Excess oxygen supports complete rather than incomplete combustion.',[RX,ORG],question='What are the products of complete combustion of a pure hydrocarbon in excess oxygen?',options=['Carbon and hydrogen','Carbon and water','Carbon monoxide and hydrogen','Carbon dioxide and water'])
yes(606,'C','The gas that gives a pop with a lighted splint is hydrogen. The oily product decolourises bromine by addition at carbon–carbon unsaturation, consistent with an alkene from cracking. The matching pair is hydrogen and alkene.',[ORG,REB,'https://myschool.ng/classroom/chemistry/13933?exam_type=jamb&exam_year=1998&page=7'],question='Cracking medicinal paraffin produces a gas that gives a pop with a lighted splint and an oily liquid that decolourises bromine solution. Which listed pair matches these observations?',options=['Carbon dioxide and an alkyne','Carbon monoxide and an alkene','Hydrogen and an alkene','Hydrogen and an alkane'],audit='Readable full Myschool13933 confirms B and C both have alkene, D alkane; removed duplicate options through exact matching recovery. Explanation treats observations as consistency with listed pair rather than exclusive proof against all unlisted unsaturated compounds.')
yes(607,'C','C₆H₅OH represents phenol, with an OH group attached to a benzene ring. That aromatic ring distinguishes it from the saturated aliphatic compounds listed.',[ORG],question='Which formula represents an aromatic compound?',options=['C₆H₁₃OH','C₆H₁₃Cl','C₆H₅OH','C₆H₁₄'],audit='Restored A from visually inspected PDF53, where the initial formula isC₆H₁₃OH, not CH₆H₁₃OH.')
yes(608,'B','The diol and dicarboxylic acid form ester links while eliminating small water molecules. Repeated condensation produces the polyester Terylene.',[CARB,REB],question='Terylene is formed from ethane-1,2-diol and benzene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid by which type of polymer-forming reaction?',options=['Addition','Condensation','Elimination','Substitution'],audit='Repaired condensation spelling and described polymer-forming classification.')
no(609,'Actual1997Q50 on PDF50. Carbonyl carbon drawn with C=O and three single bonds is pentavalent; lost/misplaced substituent cannot be resolved. Old explanation also calls tertiary2-methylbutan2ol secondary. Need intact matching oxidation-product structure.',year=1997,answer='D if intended pentan2one, unconfirmed')
yes(610,'A','Both can undergo substitution under suitable conditions. Benzene undergoes electrophilic substitution, such as nitration, while hexane undergoes radical substitution with halogens under light. Both are liquids at room temperature.',[ORG],question='Which statement is true of benzene and hexane under suitable reaction conditions?',options=['Both undergo substitution reactions','Both characteristically undergo addition reactions','Both are solids at room temperature','Both readily decolourise bromine water in the ordinary unsaturation test'],audit='Removed year heading and made normal physical/testing conditions explicit. Does not claim identical reaction mechanisms.')
yes(611,'C','A sieve separates particles by size. Smaller particles pass through its openings while larger particles are retained.',[REB],year=2000,audit='Actual2000Q2 on PDF57; stable1999ID preserved.')
yes(612,'C','At the same conditions, diffusion through a porous plug is faster for lower molar mass. The masses are propane 44, oxygen 32, methane 16 and ammonia 17 g mol⁻¹. Methane is the lightest and diffuses fastest.',[GAS],question='Which gas diffuses fastest through a porous plug under the same conditions? [H = 1, C = 12, N = 14, O = 16]',options=['Propane','Oxygen','Methane','Ammonia'],audit='Corrected old D→C; methane16 is lighter than ammonia17.',check='fastest-gas')
yes(613,'C','Clay commonly contains aluminosilicate minerals. Kaolinite, for example, has formula Al₂Si₂O₅(OH)₄, containing aluminium, silicon, oxygen and hydrogen. The other listed substances do not contain this set of elements.',['https://pubs.usgs.gov/of/2003/ofr-03-395/DESCRIPT/M/kaolinite_cm7.html',REB],year=2000,question='Which listed material contains aluminium, silicon, oxygen and hydrogen?',options=['Epsom salt','Limestone','Clay','Urea'],audit='Actual2000Q3 on PDF57; repaired Epsom spelling and avoided treating variable clay as a single pure compound.',check='kaolinite-elements')
yes(614,'B','Magnesium combines with oxygen when heated in air: 2Mg + O₂ → 2MgO. The oxygen added to the solid increases its mass. This is different from roasting a sulfide, where sulfur can leave as a gas.',[RX],question='Which listed substance gains mass by combining with oxygen when heated in air?',options=['Helium','Magnesium','Copper pyrites','Glass'],audit='Removed old blanket claim that copper pyrites does not react; magnesium mass-gain mechanism independently checked.',check='magnesium-oxidation')
yes(615,'C','The air supplies 150 × 0.20 = 30 cm³ oxygen. From 2CO + O₂ → 2CO₂, 50 cm³ CO requires 25 cm³ oxygen. Therefore oxygen is in excess by 5 cm³. Nitrogen is not a reactant here.',[RX,GAS],year=2000,question='50 cm³ of carbon monoxide reacts with 150 cm³ of air containing 20% oxygen by volume. Which reactant is in excess, comparing gas volumes at the same conditions?',options=['Carbon monoxide','Carbon dioxide','Oxygen','Nitrogen'],audit='Actual2000Q4 on PDF57; explained nitrogen is an inert diluent rather than a reactant.',check='co-excess')
yes(616,'A','At constant volume for a fixed amount of ideal gas, P/T is constant. The initial temperature is 273 K. Thus T₂ = 273 × (3/9) = 91 K.',[GAS],question='A fixed amount of ideal gas is initially at 0 °C and 9 atm. What is its final temperature if the pressure falls to 3 atm at constant volume? [0 °C = 273 K]',audit='Removed page marker and stated the standard ideal-gas model; no change to experimental values.',check='constant-volume-temperature')
yes(617,'D','The solids have different solubilities at the same temperature. Controlled cooling and successive collection of crystals can separate them into fractions. This process is fractional crystallisation.',[REB],question='Solubility curves for two solids P and Q both rise as temperature increases. P is more soluble than Q at each shown temperature. Which method uses this difference to separate their mixture into successive crystal fractions?',options=['Distillation','Fractional distillation','Crystallisation','Fractional crystallisation'],audit='Transcribed PDF54 curves and their relative order into full accessible context; specified successive fractions to identify the specific separation technique.')
yes(618,'B','Boyle’s law gives P₁V₁ = P₂V₂ at constant temperature. Hence V₂ = (1/1.5) × 300 = 200 cm³. Higher pressure gives a smaller volume.',[GAS,'https://myschool.ng/classroom/chemistry/1486?exam_type=jamb&exam_year=2000&page=1'],year=2000,question='The ratio of initial to final pressure of a fixed amount of gas is 1:1.5. What is its final volume if its initial volume is 300 cm³ at the same temperature?',options=['120 cm³','200 cm³','450 cm³','750 cm³'],audit='Actual2000Q6 on PDF57. Readable matching Myschool1486 confirms ratio1:1.5, replacing source1:1:5; corrected old D→B.',check='boyle-ratio')
yes(619,'B','The amount of HCl is 0.250 × 0.5 = 0.125 mol. Mg + 2HCl → MgCl₂ + H₂ requires half as many moles of Mg: 0.0625 mol. With Mg = 24, the mass is 0.0625 × 24 = 1.5 g.',[RX,'https://periodic-table.rsc.org/element/12/magnesium'],question='Mg(s) + 2HCl(aq) → MgCl₂(aq) + H₂(g). What mass of magnesium reacts completely with 250 cm³ of 0.5 mol dm⁻³ HCl? [Mg = 24]',options=['0.3 g','1.5 g','2.4 g','3.0 g'],audit='Removed next-question atomic-massM27 carryover/incorrect supplied Mg datum and used standard rounded magnesium atomic mass24 supported by RSC24.305. Experimental volume/concentration unchanged.',check='magnesium-acid')
yes(620,'B','Dalton’s law gives mole fraction = partial pressure / total pressure. For oxygen this is 452/780 = 0.579 to three decimal places.',[GAS],year=2000,question='A gas sample has oxygen partial pressure 452 mmHg and total pressure 780 mmHg. What is the oxygen mole fraction?',options=['0.203','0.579','2.030','5.790'],audit='Actual2000Q7 on PDF57. Described specified sample without implying59% oxygen is ordinary atmospheric air.',check='mole-fraction')
yes(621,'D','Particles in solids mainly vibrate around fixed positions; liquid particles can move past one another; gas particles move freely over much larger distances. Their freedom of movement distinguishes the states.',[REB],year=2000,audit='Actual2000Q8 on PDF57; retained complete options.')
yes(622,'D','The gaseous state has far more possible spatial arrangements than the solid or liquid states. Steam at 100 °C therefore has the greatest positional disorder among the choices. Equal temperature does not imply liquid and vapour have equal entropy.',[REB],question='In which sample do water molecules have the most disordered spatial arrangement?',options=['Ice at −10 °C','Ice at 0 °C','Liquid water at 100 °C','Steam at 100 °C'],audit='Removed false old assertion that steam at same temperature has greater mean kinetic energy; explanation distinguishes arrangement/entropy.')
no(623,'Actual2000Q9 on PDF57. Progressive1-to8 outer-electron pattern is not universal across transition-element periods, and C omits same/different before number of shells. Hold complete options and valid scope rather than restrict the period to force B.',year=2000,answer='B intended main-group trend')
yes(624,'B','First ionisation energy is the energy required to remove one electron from each gaseous neutral atom in one mole, forming gaseous singly charged ions. For sodium, that electron is in the 3s orbital.',[REB],question='Removing one 3s electron from each atom in one mole of gaseous sodium atoms requires about 496 kJ. What is this energy called?',options=['Electron affinity','Ionisation energy','Activation energy','Electronegativity'])
yes(625,'C','A suitable ion-exchange resin removes hardness-causing Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺ ions by replacing them with ions such as Na⁺. Chlorination and aeration do not perform this exchange.',[REB],question='Which method can soften hard water?',options=['Chlorination','Passing over activated charcoal','Using an ion-exchange resin','Aeration'],audit='Restored B boundary from PDF54 and removed next-question table instruction.')
no(626,'PDF54 recovered table: W GroupI period3, X GroupIV period2, Y GroupVII period3, Z Group0 period2. X carbon is intendedC, but Y chlorine also commonly participates in covalent bonding; unqualified likely criterion lacks bonding partner/comparison. Keep complete mapping privately for later source clarification.',answer='C intended carbon; Y also covalent')
yes(627,'D','Z occupies Group 0, the noble-gas group, and has a complete outer electron shell. It is therefore the least reactive of the labelled elements. W is an alkali metal, X is carbon and Y is a halogen.',[REB],question='Four positions in a periodic-table section are labelled: W is in Group I, period 3; X in Group IV, period 2; Y in Group VII, period 3; and Z in Group 0, period 2. Which element is least reactive?',options=['W','X','Y','Z'],audit='PDF54 periodic-table image visually inspected; restored allfour labelled positions. Z is noble gas, not W. Corrected oldA→D; source group numbering retained.',check='noble-gas-mapping')
no(628,'Configuration is cobalt, a transition element and also a metal. OptionsB andC therefore both true. Hold for most-specific classification criterion or nonoverlapping options rather than claim metal is false.',answer='C most specific, B also true')
yes(629,'A','Fluorine is very small and highly electronegative, making the H–F bond strongly polar and allowing strong intermolecular hydrogen bonding. Hydrogen bonding in NH₃ is weaker; CH₄ and HCl lack the strong conventional N–H, O–H or F–H pattern.',[REB,'https://myschool.ng/classroom/chemistry/842?exam_type=jamb&page=12'],question='Which listed compound has the strongest intermolecular hydrogen bonding, considering atomic size and electronegativity?',options=['HF','NH₃','CH₄','HCl'],audit='Readable Myschool842 confirms missing NH₃ subscript. Removed gas-phase tags from general intermolecular comparison without asserting an extended liquid network in isolated gas molecules.')
yes(630,'A','The HCl concentration is 0.25/0.50 = 0.50 mol dm⁻³. The 15.00 cm³ portion contains 0.00750 mol HCl. Since Na₂CO₃ + 2HCl → 2NaCl + CO₂ + H₂O, the carbonate amount is 0.00375 mol. Dividing by 0.01250 dm³ gives 0.30 mol dm⁻³.',[RX],question='0.25 mol HCl is dissolved and made up to 0.50 dm³. A 15.00 cm³ portion neutralises 12.50 cm³ of aqueous sodium carbonate completely. What is the carbonate concentration?',options=['0.30 mol dm⁻³','0.40 mol dm⁻³','0.50 mol dm⁻³','0.60 mol dm⁻³'],audit='Corrected oldB→A by exact carbonate stoichiometry; repaired carbonate name.',check='carbonate-concentration')
yes(631,'A','Oxygen is −2 and potassium +1. In V₂O₅, vanadium is +5; in K₂Cr₂O₇, chromium is +6; and in KMnO₄, manganese is +7. The increasing order is therefore V₂O₅, K₂Cr₂O₇, KMnO₄.',[RX],question='Which order gives increasing oxidation number of the transition elements in these compounds?',options=['V₂O₅ < K₂Cr₂O₇ < KMnO₄','K₂Cr₂O₇ < KMnO₄ < V₂O₅','KMnO₄ < K₂Cr₂O₇ < V₂O₅','KMnO₄ < V₂O₅ < K₂Cr₂O₇'],audit='Restored formula subscripts and inequality punctuation from PDF54.',check='oxidation-order')
no(632,'Plastic composition unspecified; HCl requires chlorine-containing polymers, while many plastics contain no chlorine. Petrol composition and burning conditions also affect emissions. Hold exact source question rather than add PVC as a new premise to force C.')
yes(633,'D','Acidified iodate oxidises iodide to iodine: IO₃⁻ + 5I⁻ + 6H⁺ → 3I₂ + 3H₂O. Dissolved iodine, including triiodide in excess iodide, gives the reddish-brown colour.',[RX,'https://edu.rsc.org/in-search-of-more-solutions/a-chemical-stop-clock-iodine-clock-reaction/598.article','https://myschool.ng/classroom/chemistry/1380?exam_type=jamb&page=12'],question='What is observed when aqueous sulfuric acid, potassium iodate(V) and potassium iodide are mixed?',options=['A white precipitate forms','A green precipitate forms','The mixture remains colourless','The mixture becomes reddish-brown'],audit='Readable full Myschool1380 confirms potassium trioxoiodate(V) and iodide, restoring garbled species. Corrected old explanation: iodate, not sulfuric acid alone, is the oxidant.',check='iodate-iodide')
no(634,'PDF54 graph shows about10mol/dm³ at80°C and8 at60°C, formally2mol×58.5=117g in a constant1dm³ model, not old5.85g. These values are not a realistic NaCl solubility curve and final-solution volume is not specified. Hold for correct source graph/identity and volume convention rather than adopt fabricated generic values.',answer='A formal fixed-volume graph calculation only')
no(635,'All four portions have the same written HCl concentration m/n, so their pH values are equal; volume alone does not change concentration. Need distinct original concentrations or a mixing premise. Old answer contradicts its own explanation; no unique listed option.')
yes(636,'C','Let the molar solubility be s. Dissociation gives [Cu²⁺] = s and [IO₃⁻] = 2s. Hence Ksp = s(2s)² = 4s³ = 1.08 × 10⁻⁷. Therefore s = ∛(2.70 × 10⁻⁸) = 3.0 × 10⁻³ mol dm⁻³.',[REB,'https://myschool.ng/classroom/chemistry/1391'],question='Cu(IO₃)₂ has Ksp = 1.08 × 10⁻⁷. Assuming negligible reaction of its ions with water, what is its molar solubility in pure water?',options=['2.7 × 10⁻⁸ mol dm⁻³','9.0 × 10⁻⁸ mol dm⁻³','3.0 × 10⁻³ mol dm⁻³','9.0 × 10⁻³ mol dm⁻³'],audit='Readable full Myschool1391 confirms C and D exponents−3, removing duplicated−8 source options. Independent cubic solution and back-substitution.',check='iodate-solubility')
yes(637,'A','Entropy describes the number of accessible microscopic arrangements, often introduced as disorder. Enthalpy is H = U + pV; its change equals heat transferred at constant pressure when only pressure–volume work occurs. The introductory pairing is disorder and heat content, respectively.',[REB,'https://openstax.org/books/chemistry-2e/pages/5-3-enthalpy'],question='In introductory thermodynamic terminology, entropy and enthalpy are associated respectively with',options=['Degree of disorder and heat content','Heat content and degree of disorder','Heat content only','Degree of disorder only'],audit='Retained introductory association while explanation supplies preciseH definition and constant-pressure limitation.')
yes(638,'C','Vanadium(V) oxide is the catalyst used in the Contact process for converting SO₂ to SO₃. It provides a faster reaction pathway without changing the equilibrium composition.',[CAT,REB,'https://myschool.ng/classroom/chemistry/1396'],question='For 2SO₂(g) + O₂(g) ⇌ 2SO₃(g), which listed catalyst increases the rate of sulfur trioxide production in the Contact process?',options=['Manganese(IV) oxide','Finely divided iron','Vanadium(V) oxide','Nickel'],audit='Readable full Myschool1396 explicitly restores SO₃ product instead of printedNO₂, plus iron and vanadiumV names. No chemistry-driven species substitution without matching source.',check='contact-balance')
yes(639,'B','Forming one mole of O₂ requires transfer of four moles of electrons. For 0.125 mol O₂, the charge is 0.125 × 4 × 96,500 = 48,250 C.',[EL],question='What charge is required to liberate 0.125 mol O₂ during electrolysis of dilute sodium chloride solution, assuming full current efficiency for oxygen evolution? [F = 96,500 C mol⁻¹]',options=['24,125 C','48,250 C','72,375 C','96,500 C'],audit='Corrected old D→B; no extra full-reaction multiplier.',check='oxygen-charge')
yes(640,'B','For the same metal and current efficiency, deposited mass is proportional to charge It. The second charge is (2I × 10)/(I × 40) = 1/2 of the first, so the deposited mass is X/2 g.',[EL],question='Current I deposits X g of a univalent metal in 40 minutes. What mass is deposited by current 2I in 10 minutes under the same conditions?',options=['X/4 g','X/2 g','2X g','4X g'],audit='Restored current symbolI rather than digit1; same electrolysis conditions explicit.',check='current-time-ratio')
